    "body": "Trying to write this out, so far I got as far as loading up rhai crate in my starship fork on Windows.  Every journey begins with a single step:. :)\r\n\r\nAnyway I guess the next bit is to figure out where I can jam this thing in.  But it needs to override `format` somehow or at least pass some sort of value so that I can do\r\n\r\nformat=$rhai_output\r\n\r\nSeems like `line_break.rs` is the best module to start from it's less than 50 lines\r\n\r\n--- \r\nwell that didn't work out.  I can't get it to compile :)  then again I didn't expect much this is basically my first Rust program :)\r\n\r\n```\r\nuse super::{Context, Module};\r\nuse crate::segment::Segment;\r\nuse rhai::{Engine, EvalAltResult};\r\n\r\n/// Creates a module for the line break\r\npub fn module<'a>(context: &'a Context) -> Option<Module<'a>> {\r\n\r\n    let mut module = context.new_module(\"rhai\");\r\n\r\n    let engine = Engine::new();\r\n\r\n    //let result = engine.eval::<i64>(\"40 + 2\")?;\r\n    let result = engine.eval::<String>(\"40 + 2\")?;\r\n\r\n    module.set_segments(vec![Segment::new(None, result)]);\r\n\r\n    Some(module)\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nbut the general idea I have now is instead of replacing \"format\" just add a new variable called `$rhai` which will contain the output of a script in the above I tried to make it store the value of 42, but I can change the eval from a string to an external file.\r\n\r\nHaving this basically turns starship to a general purpose rhai script engine  though we can probably add context values in there for the modules that are already present in starship.\r\n\r\nAt least this approach allows backwards compatibility with Starship's existing code base just adds some great power and hopefully great responsibility for the rhai script creator.",
