    "body": "@Sylvain303\r\nI would like to throw my 2 cents:\r\nShell scripts are hardly readable, maintainability is very poor. As @davidkna said, they are not platform independent, even distribution independent on Linux. One distro might have some old version of bash and custom script breaks. Also, the big problem is that bash features are mostly not POSIX complaint so it is hard to port it to systems that do not use Bash.\r\n\r\nUsing anything else than built in scripting engine makes custom modules platform dependent, be it either by using script which needs interpreter or binary that was built on specific hardware and system.\r\n\r\nStarship is truly platform independent only when it comes to built in modules. However, due to increasing popularity of this project, it is not feasible to add every single user-invented module to core Starship because of limited capacity of development team. Embedding scripting language is beneficial to both side, developers and users of Starship:\r\n - users get easy, platform independent way to customize Starship behavior\r\n - developers can focus on core functionality instead of reviewing and managing PRs with custom modules",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
