    "body": "I would like to pick up the discussion here and see if this is something that might be worth adding. If yes, I could work on it. \r\n\r\nIMO compared to `custom` modules internal scripting would have the advantage of: \r\n- speed (not only script execution, but the additional shell startup time to execute the command + the `when` command). While the improvement might be in the milliseconds depending on the setup. \r\n- portability of scripts \r\n- readability / accessibility for new developers (while that might be personal preference)\r\n- **ability to expose starship-internal functionality to the script**. For example the context, cached information of the git repository, `exec_cmd` and with its timeouts. (this is standard in the lua-world with UserData)\r\n- Ability to return structured data. For example multiple keys / values that then can be used in the `format` string.  \r\n\r\nIn the end since most modules (that I saw) are just reading files or the environment, or executing commands and many could actually be (theoretically) replaced with a script. \r\n\r\n\r\nFor the sake of not reinventing the wheel I believe lua (probably with the [`mlua`](https://docs.rs/mlua/latest/mlua/) or  [`rlua`](https://docs.rs/rlua/latest/rlua/) crate) is the best choice for this. Many devs are already familiar, and we have existing tooling for the language. Furthermore it even has a small standard library while being small ( 120kb binary) and fast. It's mature and is widely adopted (in gaming for example, or neovim). \r\n\r\nsmall edit: \r\nwe could also expose the module configuration to the script, to make these more portable across users. \r\n\r\nanother edit: \r\nI would imagine this similar to the custom module, with separate scripts for each use-case.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
