    "created_at": "2022-04-06T06:51:59Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-04-06T06:51:59Z",
    "body": "I'll dare to provide some example. I'll use Lua because:\r\n- I like it\r\n- it is almost as readable as Python\r\n- it was designed as embedded-able scripting language\r\n\r\nI believe that each plugin should exist as a separate file. That will improve maintainability in comparison to central config file.\r\n\r\nSo here it comes - it is example of custom Go module:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal sh = requires(\"starship-helpers/v1\")\r\n\r\n--- this is description of plugin\r\n\r\nfunction when()\r\n    if sh.file_exists(sh.getcwd() .. \"go.mod\") \r\n    then\r\n        return true\r\n    end\r\n    return false\r\nend\r\n\r\nfunction symbol()\r\n    return \"🐹\"\r\nend\r\n\r\nfunction style()\r\n    return \"bold cyan\"\r\nend\r\n\r\nfunction format()\r\n    return \"[$symbol$output]($style)\"\r\nend\r\n```\r\nI made different fields from config functions to provide better dynamic behaviour in case of need - user always can return just static value. Or maybe it would be better to return some Starship specific object instead of a string?\r\n\r\nLua's standard library is kinda small so it would be natural for Starship to provide helper libraries with additional functionality - not only access to internal Starship attributes but also for things like file format parsers of library to interact with Git, for example. \r\nAs you can see in example, `starship-helpers` library is versioned - that will make life easier in future in case of breaking changes. Probably it is better to split it in different libraries.\r\n\r\nAs for Rust part, there's rlua and mlua libraries that provide bindings to C Lua implementation (which is less than ideal) but I was not able to find pure Rust Lua implementation.",
