    "created_at": "2022-04-06T07:59:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-04-06T07:59:35Z",
    "body": "My first idea actually was something else, while your approach has this advantage of supporting shared code between `when` and the actual module. \r\n\r\n```toml \r\n[script.aws]\r\nscript = \"\"\"\r\nlocal aws_profile = context.get_env(\"AWS_PROFILE\")\r\nlocal config_file = context.read_ini_file(context.get_env(\"AWS_CONFIG_FILE\") or (context.get_home() .. \".aws/config\"))\r\n\r\nlocal region = config_file and (\r\n    config_file[\"profile \" .. aws_profile][\"region\"] or config_file[\"default\"][\"region\"]\r\n)\r\n\r\nfrom_config = context.get_config(\"some_abitary_config\")\r\n\r\nreturn {\r\n    profile = profile,\r\n    region = region,\r\n    some_config = from_config,\r\n}\r\n\r\n\"\"\"\r\nwhen = true\r\nsymbol = \" \"\r\nformat = \"[($symbol $region $profile )]($style)\"\r\nstyle = \"purple\"\r\nsome_abitary_config = \"some_value\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nNote the `region` from the returned table is used in the `format` string and we can access config. \r\nThere probably could be more file-format specific helpers (toml, yaml, json, ...) that simply convert into lua tables. The [lua stdlib is small](https://www.lua.org/manual/5.3/manual.html#6) so there probably would be more helpers added over time, similar to what neovim does (`.exec_cmd` for example to handle timeouts). Simple cases like reading env or files are possible. \r\n\r\nSome platform specific things might be module-specific and need to stay, but where we find ways we can ease the way for script devs (for example with wrapping a `PathBuf` and expose it to the script). \r\n\r\nI'm not sure if versioning is needed, but could be done too if needed. By using `require` people could even call external files, or use [luarocks](https://luarocks.org/) modules. \r\n\r\nI'm not 100% certain yet how error handling could work in a nice way in this script, without polluting the lua-code too much. Inside starship we often use `Option<T>` and just return `None` on most errors, which roughly matches what lua does most of the time (return a tuple `nil, \"error message\"` on error, and just `return_value` on success). But IMO this can be figured out when building the engine. We could also _limit_ the functionality the script has, like removing  `os.getenv`, `io.popen` or `os.execute`)\r\n\r\nPersonally I don't think styles, format and symbols need to come from lua, but that might be only me. ",
